A||||6||GT Bastard||AutoX questions...||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-05-2000||07:51 PM||||I need to make a choice here. I installed a set of new Monroe roadhandler shocks and struts last fall, and now I'm planning to autox the car.  Before I do, I'm investing in a set of spring from ST or Eibach.  now, I've heard that it's not a good idea to mix hard springs with softer shocks and struts... But does anybody know how hard Monroe roadhandlers are???  I mean, they tightened up the cars responses, but would this be a bad idea to mix?  suggestions are appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-06-2000||12:39 AM||||Race first and get a baseline for performance.  Then modifications can be documented with performance changes, really hard to have too much info.<BR>As a general rule stiffer shocks and softer springs work better than vice versa, but the springs have to match the car's weight and the shocks need to match both sprung and unsprung weights.  Engines are simple compared to suspensions, less variables.<BR>Hard springs for the street are different than race only springs, ditto for shocks.  The combo you have should work together better than either would if paired to stock.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||02-06-2000||11:45 PM||||I am currently trying/testing Eibach and KYB combination I like them so far but it is a hard ride. I live by a very big abandoned parking lot and did a few AutoX manuevers, WOW what a differance it's a whole new game now! way more controlable.(point it were you want to go and floor it!) KYB's are considered to be medium soft shocks. One other consideration the Eibachs don't sit in their spring perches when jacked up this makes me nervous. I've recieved one reply [Eibach vs. ST's] saying the ST's sit in the perch. So far I've looked for stuff to test if it will pop out in normal driving conditions, not even a poping noise yet! I would like to hear from more people on this subject.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||02-07-2000||02:30 AM||||Good info guys.... Do you think I'll be ok with the monroe stuff?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phaeton||02-07-2000||04:22 AM||||If you drive the Fiero daily and autoX on weekends the roadhandlers are ok, but a tad soft for racing.  If the Fiero is going to be primarily an autoX racer then go get some racing shocks.  Racing shocks don't have enough give to take one of those good potholes found on public streets, tend to bend things quicker.  I personally run offroad shocks, but... on poor roads a long line of cars forms behind me, some of them are rude even.  But I can't speed up 'cause my shocks are made for fast and smooth.  What's your frustration level?||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||02-07-2000||07:08 PM||||Parking lots are smooth,and autocsrossing is about technique,finesse and control,so the shocks won't make as much difference as swaybars,springs,tire pressures.<BR>I've used Monroe shocks before,I felt that they had a poor response time for performance road use,love my Bilsteins,even on poor Detroit roads,Konis worked about as good as the ones I have,just rode worse.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||02-07-2000||08:53 PM||||Do you guys think the ST's or the Eibachs's would work better with my Monroe stuff? I'm leaning towards to ST's, but any advice? ||||reg||1
